actors; confirms negative association between albuminuria and BMI and reports positive associations.)\n\n4) Hofmeyr GJ. \"Abdominal decompression for suspected fetal compromise/pre-eclampsia.\" Cochrane Database Syst Rev. 2012 Jun 13;(6):CD000004. doi:10.1002/14651858.CD000004.pub2. PMID: 22696313. Free PMC article. (Cochrane review: abdominal decompression originally for labour pain; evaluated for pregnancy complications and fetal wellbeing/intellectual development; objectives and evidence summary provided.)\n\n5) Logie JJ et al. \"Evaluation of kisspeptin levels in obese pregnancy as a biomarker for pre-eclampsia.\" Clin Endocrinol (Oxf). 2012 Jun;76(6):887-893. doi:10.1111/j.1365-2265.2011.04317.x. PMID: 22168508. (Prospective longitudinal study in UK antenatal metabolic clinic: severely obese (BMI >40 kg/m2, n=194) vs lean (BMI <25 kg/m2, n=78); assessed kisspeptin as biomarker for pre-eclampsia.)\n\n6) Thompson AM, Bizzarro MJ. \"Necrotizing enterocolitis in newborns: pathogenesis, prevention and management.\" Drugs. 2008;68(9):1227-1238. doi:10.2165/00003495-200868090-00004. PMID: 18547133. (Review: clinical signs include feeding intolerance, emesis, bloody stools, abdominal distention/tenderness; lab indicators of infection/coagulation/fluid retention.)\n\n7) Vaidya A et al. \"Effects of antenatal multiple micronutrient supplementation on children's weight and size at 2 years of age in Nepal: follow-up of a double-blind randomized controlled trial.\" Lancet. 2008 Feb 9;371(9611):492-499. doi:10.1016/S0140-6736(08)60172-5. PMID: 18262041. Free PMC article. (Clinical trial follow-up: systolic blood pressure was slightly lower in the intervention group by 2.5 mm Hg [95% CI 0.5\u20134.6]; interpretation in a low-resource population.)\n\n8) Hofmeyr GJ. \"Abdominal decompression for suspected fetal compromise/pre-eclampsia.\" Cochrane Database Syst Rev. 2000;(2):CD000004. doi:10.1002/14651858.CD000004. PMID: 10796079. (Earlier version of Cochrane review; updated in