          "status": "GREEN",
          "disclaimer": "Notice: This snippet is extracted from the open access paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.2337/diacare.1.1.49?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.2337/diacare.1.1.49, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."
        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "First, urinary estriol excretion is highly correlated with birth weight. Urinary estriol determination should therefore also correlate with the results of ultrasonography. Not only is this true, but some have even suggested that the latter provides a better indicator of fetal well-being and a better predictor of perinatal asphyxia than estriol levels. 81 Secondly, urinary estriol excretion in patients with diabetes mellitus, con-sidered as a group, is more variable than in normal pregnant patients. Therefore, values obtained from patients with diabetes should not be compared with those from normal patients who serve as the usual laboratory controls. Thirdly, the day-to-day variability in estriol excretion in individual patients is substantial. Thus, for a fall in estriol excretion to be judged significant (greater than 35 per cent), it requires comparison with the antecedent stable excretion in the individual patient, not in other patients with diabetes complicating pregnancy. A serious drawback to this requirement is the narrow margin separating day-to-day variations in estriol excretion from apparent \"significant\" falls. Lastly, estriol excretion, if it is to be of value, must be determined daily, not twice or three times a week, as is commonly practiced. Otherwise, significant falls in excretion may be missed entirely or identified with too great a delay to be of value. If these criteria of daily estriol assays from the 32nd week of gestation onward, performed in a reliable laboratory with a rapid turn-around time, cannot be met, then estriol determinations should not be done at all. \n\nThe most important advance in recent years in the management of pregnancies complicated by diabetes mellitus has been the introduction of amniotic fluid phospholipid determinations. 87 \" 92 In distinction to surveillance procedures for fetal well-being and jeopardy, the serial assessment of amniotic fluid lecithin/sphingomyelin ratio (L/S ratio), addresses the issue of fetal lung maturation.",
