        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "36 weeks (Doubilet et al., 2003, p. 360). They identified reduced size in all dimensions (biparietal diameter, abdominal circumference and femur diaphyseal length) among the very premature fetuses in the first interval, while a decline characterized the later-delivering preterm fetuses. Abdominal circumference and femur length were reduced during the first interval, joined by biparietal diameter after 30 weeks.\n\nThus, the data collection protocol in the present sample enabled identification of the overall growth pattern. In general, it is likely that both small fetal size and large fetal size are precursors of preterm delivery, as both are relatively aberrant patterns. This idea is compatible with previous observations of both under-and over-growth among samples of pregnancies ending in spontaneous preterm labor and birth (Lackman et al., 2001;Morken et al., 2006). The present data are unique in illustrating the less appreciated influence of the large fetal size.",
