  "title": "Approaches to Craniosynostosis",
  "content": "Eric H. Hubli, MD, FACS, FAAP, FICS, serves as guest editor for this issue of Seminars in Plastic Surgery, devoted to craniosynostosis. Key points and contributions in the issue:\n\n- Theme: modern craniofacial surgery aims not merely for an obvious \u201cresult\u201d but for a normal, natural appearance that blends with the community\u2014ideally so inconspicuous that no one recognizes surgery occurred.\n\n- Historical context: craniofacial deformity management has evolved from medieval Barber surgeons through 20th-century pioneers to current, more refined approaches.\n\n- Metopic synostosis: authors advocate that the standard complete bandeau removal is not always necessary\u2014\u201csometimes \u2018more is less\u2019\u201d\u2014and less extensive approaches can achieve excellent aesthetic results.\n\n- Coronal suture closure/orbital issues (Britto et al): early coronal closure produces challenging deformities, with orbital dystopia being the most vexing component. Cranial vault repair typically involves bandeau advancement and limited frontal bone reshaping; Britto et al outline surgical options for orbital management.\n\n- Sagittal synostosis: multiple surgical paradigms exist for scaphocephaly; the issue highlights a \u201cfunctional aesthetic\u201d approach that aims to improve both form and function.\n\n- Lambdoid synostosis (Rhodes, Tye, Fearon): early lambdoid suture closure is rare\u2014the lambdoid is the least frequently affected cranial suture\u2014making this contribution particularly important for craniofacial practitioners.\n\n- Strip craniectomy with endoscopy and helmeting (Honeycutt): this approach uses endoscopic technique plus postoperative orthotic helmeting to reduce operative time, transfusion rates, and length of stay while maximizing aesthetic outcomes.\n\n- Virtual surgical planning (Chim, Wetjen, Mardini): computer-a...
